          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01KqvQEm64pNq1rDUwz3MXzt",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense semantic search to improve retrieval quality and relevance.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Primary source describes hybrid retrieval as production-proven; single independent source.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines two complementary search approaches to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity:\\n\\n- **Sparse lexical search** (e.g. BM25): Excels at matching exact terms, product identifiers (SKUs), error codes, and other rare lexical features.\\n- **Dense vector search**: Captures semantic similarity and intent, finding results that are conceptually related even if they lack exact term overlap.\\n\\nThe two ranked result sets are then merged, often via **reciprocal rank fusion**, to produce a final ranking that balances both signals.\\n\\n### Performance and Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries that mix rare identifiers with natural language intent. It has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a merging technique used in hybrid retrieval (and other multi-stage ranking scenarios) to combine rankings from independent systems. It avoids needing to normalize scores across differently-scaled systems and is known for its robustness.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-05\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
